WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.520
Hello, everyone.

9
00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:25.940
Hello, Jenna.

10
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.320
Hello, everybody.

11
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.920
Today, we're breaking down The Sting.

12
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:35.680
It is Season 7, Episode 5, written by Mindy Kaling and directed by Randall Einhorn.

13
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.360
I'm excited for this one.

14
00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.660
I'm excited for so many reasons.

15
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:40.240
I know.

16
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:40.660
Me too.

17
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:42.200
Well, let me start with a summary.

18
00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:48.340
A handsome salesman from a rival paper company has been stealing Dunder Mifflin's clients.

19
00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.900
So Michael stages an elaborate sting operation to learn his secrets.

20
00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:03.000
Meanwhile, Andy starts a band with Daryl and Kevin after learning his less-talented former classmate, Broccoli Rob, has had a taste of musical success.

21
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:04.640
Kelsey Yum!

22
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:09.500
And Broccoli Rob has a commercial called Kelsey Yum!

23
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.180
For the milk company.

24
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.780
Yeah, which cracked me up because Phyllis was like, you know what?

25
00:01:13.900 --> 00:01:15.080
That's a really good reminder.

26
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:16.040
I need to drink milk.

27
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.760
You know, I was the voice of, like, cheese, Wisconsin cheese for a year.

28
00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:22.780
What was your catchphrase?

29
00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:31.800
I can't remember if we had a catchphrase, but for a year, I recorded commercials for Wisconsin cheese and talked about grilled cheese sandwiches.

30
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:33.440
That's awesome.

31
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.020
I wonder if they exist anywhere.

32
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:36.220
That makes me so happy.

33
00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:43.120
Well, listen, today, our fast facts are once again, for the third week in a row, an interview.

34
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.120
You guys, we are so excited.

35
00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:47.540
Guess who's here?

36
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.240
We've been wanting this for a long time.

37
00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:50.440
It's Craig Robinson!

38
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.200
Oh my gosh.

39
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:54.420
We have been wanting this for a long time.

40
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.700
He is busy, but he is here today, and I am so excited.

41
00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.960
And we are going to ask him everything.

42
00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.220
Who is this?

43
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.580
Who's on my screen right now?

44
00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.900
Hello, office ladies!

45
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:13.340
Hi, Craig!

46
00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:14.760
What's up, Craig?

47
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:15.780
How's it going?

48
00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:17.100
It's going good.

49
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.380
Oh my God, it's so good to see your face.

50
00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:20.080
Likewise.

51
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:21.860
What's up over there?

52
00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:22.920
What is this I'm looking at?

53
00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:26.680
You're looking at our office ladies studio here at Earwolf.

54
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.460
With lots of lamps, because we can't see anymore.

55
00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.080
Yeah, we each get our own lamp at office ladies.

56
00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:32.620
Yeah.

57
00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:34.680
Only the beSt.

58
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:35.580
Only the best!

59
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:36.940
Very nice.

60
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:38.140
Very nice.

61
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.120
Well, Craig, we're going to jump right in.

62
00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.160
We always ask our guests, how did they get their job on the office?

63
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.300
I auditioned.

64
00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:51.300
It was Greg Daniels, and then there must have been 12 other people in the room,

65
00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:54.420
and I read one of the confessionals.

66
00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.600
Yeah, the talking heads to camera.

67
00:02:56.780 --> 00:02:58.160
I read one of the talking heads.

68
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:04.240
And the beauty of my audition, the beauty and also terror, was when I walked in.

69
00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:07.780
At that time, I had a video out with Jerry Minor.

70
00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.700
It was a song called Somebody's Fing My Lady.

71
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:12.760
Yes, very familiar.

72
00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:13.480
We know it.

73
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.120
You know, making rounds and stuff.

74
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:20.060
And, you know, this is, obviously, people look at your work before you come in to see

75
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.520
what they're dealing with kind of thing.

76
00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:24.360
But at that time, I didn't realize people were, like, looking at your work.

77
00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:25.580
I just thought you just went in.

78
00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.540
And Greg Daniels was like, I saw the song.

79
00:03:29.620 --> 00:03:30.900
It doesn't get any funnier than that.

80
00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:34.680
And so that could either, you could take that two ways.

81
00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.160
Hey, you know, you're in, you know, and just go have a good time.

82
00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.460
Or, wow, you're going to really mess this up now.

83
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:43.700
Yeah, because you had peaked already.

84
00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:44.880
Yeah, exactly.

85
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:46.540
So I was like, okay.

86
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:53.780
But thankfully, you know, they laughed and came out as Daryl.

87
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.240
Now, when you got the role as Daryl, did you know that it was going to be recurring?

88
00:03:58.680 --> 00:03:59.920
Had they already decided that?

89
00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:04.520
No, I don't think all of that was out there yet.

90
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.200
I do not know, to be perfectly honeSt.

91
00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:08.860
But, you know, I didn't pop up.

92
00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:09.900
It was only six episodes.

93
00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.500
I popped up in episode four, just to establish character.

94
00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:15.840
And then episode five was basketball.

95
00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:18.240
We didn't know what the show was going to do yet, you know?

96
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:18.920
Yeah, yeah.

97
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:20.400
Those were the early, early days.

98
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:21.140
Yeah, yeah.

99
00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.620
I remember the buzz on set when you did that introductory scene with Steve down in the warehouse.

100
00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.160
Where Michael comes up to you and he introduces you.

101
00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:38.200
And I just remember everybody being like, what has just happened?

102
00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:39.980
What did we just get?

103
00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:47.320
Because Daryl, the way Daryl looks at Michael, like, just all of that chemistry between those two characters, we hadn't had yet on the show.

104
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:48.820
And people flipped out.

105
00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:50.960
Wow, I had no idea.

106
00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:54.460
I was just staring, you know, trying to do a good job.

107
00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.080
I didn't know there was buzz.

108
00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:57.740
There was buzz, Craig.

109
00:04:58.080 --> 00:04:59.620
There was tons of buzz.

110
00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.400
Well, you know, we're rewatching this show now.

111
00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.500
For the first time, we're seeing these episodes we haven't seen in a long time.

112
00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:14.600
And, Craig, I lost my mind in episode Employee Transfer when Daryl is helping Michael and Holly move to Nashua.

113
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:16.300
I could not stop laughing.

114
00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.000
I was laughing so hard, I was crying.

115
00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.900
It's hands down one of my favorite episodes.

116
00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:30.120
And Amy Ryan was on, and she said doing those scenes with you and Steve was, quote, I'm quoting Amy Ryan, one of the highlights of her time on The Office.

117
00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.800
I called her my super, we were super friends.

118
00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:36.140
Shout out to Amy.

119
00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.860
Yeah, that was hysterical riding down there.

120
00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.740
I don't know if you noticed, my voice at that time was a little, I needed surgery.

121
00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:47.060
On my voice, I had a polyp.

122
00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:52.140
It was crazy riding with those two.

123
00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:55.820
You know, anytime you had a scene with Michael, it was like, oh, here we go.

124
00:05:55.820 --> 00:05:58.740
So they had the three of us riding down there.

125
00:05:59.020 --> 00:06:00.100
And it was just ridiculous.

126
00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:01.040
What did he say?

127
00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.120
He woke up, and she was crying.

128
00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.020
He was like, did Daryl touch you?

129
00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:07.360
Yeah.

130
00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.300
Well, do you have a favorite episode from your time on The Office?

131
00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:16.680
I know that's such a big question, and people ask us that, and we have so many.

132
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.420
But is there a moment that really stands out as one of your favorites?

133
00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:27.180
You know, the Lady Clothes episode, I thought, was when he, Negotiation, I think it was called.

134
00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:27.900
Uh-huh.

135
00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:32.780
But I had to talk Mike into going to ask for a raise so I can get a raise.

136
00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:34.780
And it turned out he got on Lady Clothes.

137
00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:35.460
Yes.

138
00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:38.880
That was, that's a standout for me.

139
00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:47.540
And also, you know, a scene stands out a lot that I really love was when Dwight lied and said he went to the dentiSt.

140
00:06:48.980 --> 00:06:52.540
And Michael was like, what's the name of the dentist?

141
00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.500
He was like, CrentiSt.

142
00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:03.760
But the way that it was shot and it felt like a Western and you really felt the tension, that's one of the standouts for me.

143
00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.460
Well, the cast used to love it when you would play music in an episode.

144
00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:10.660
We would all end up singing together.

145
00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:16.940
We know your mom was a music teacher, and she plays organ and was first chair cello player.

146
00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:17.760
Mm-hmm.

147
00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.000
Was she your first music teacher?

148
00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:20.720
Yes.

149
00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:22.140
Absolutely.

150
00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.620
Matter of fact, you know, I like to say, we learned in the womb.

151
00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:28.920
You know, she was always playing and singing to us.

152
00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:32.120
And then, yeah, then she started to teach us.

153
00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:35.540
But it was kind of funny, her teaching.

154
00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:36.140
I don't know.

155
00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:41.320
So we ended up going to like several different teachers growing up, but she definitely was the firSt.

156
00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.840
What was the first instrument you learned?

157
00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:44.720
Piano.

158
00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.160
And how many instruments do you play?

159
00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:48.760
I dabble in other instruments.

160
00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:57.120
I can figure out things on, you know, the bass or the drums or guitar or saxophone trumpet.

161
00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:03.040
I dabble, but I wouldn't dare say I play, you know, and I would insult actual players.

162
00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:04.780
But the piano, that's my thing.

163
00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:06.260
Okay.

164
00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:15.100
So in this week's episode, The Sting, Daryl, Andy, and Kevin all joined together to play Andy's original song, Please, Mr. President.

165
00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:19.600
And this sets off a really fun arc for you guys playing together.

166
00:08:19.960 --> 00:08:25.960
We did some research, Craig, and you guys play in Pam's Replacement, The Last Day in Florida.

167
00:08:25.960 --> 00:08:28.540
You also play in local ad.

168
00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:31.460
You sing everyone's favorite, people's person's paper people.

169
00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:37.860
And then your band, The Nasty Delicious with your brother, Chris Robb, played in Goodbye Tobey Part 2.

170
00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:43.760
I mean, you got to do so many cool, like, performances within the show.

171
00:08:43.960 --> 00:08:48.380
Was there one of the songs you guys performed together that was your favorite or a standout memory?

172
00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:52.920
No, that Goodbye Tobey episode was wonderful.

173
00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:56.580
Well, first, you know, I got to put my band in there and my brother.

174
00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:01.200
And then, you know, we would just be featured up there like that.

175
00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:09.980
If you watch that episode, though, I'm going to give away something probably nobody else would notice.

176
00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:17.680
But there's one part where the guitar player is just jamming, but you don't hear any guitar.

177
00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:21.480
He's just, like, jamming out.

178
00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.420
And so, you know, of course, I'm going to notice that.

179
00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.360
But that Goodbye Tobey was pretty special.

180
00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:30.920
I had to learn a song on the spot.

181
00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:32.480
I had to learn that song.

182
00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:34.480
And then I was like, oh, that's right.

183
00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:35.540
Goodbye Tobey.

184
00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:37.700
So, luckily, I was familiar with it.

185
00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:40.280
But, yeah, that was cool.

186
00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.600
That would happen to you and Ed a lot.

187
00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:42.920
Yeah.

188
00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:49.420
We talked to Ed on the podcast, and he said, you guys rarely got very much time or notice to rehearse.

189
00:09:49.700 --> 00:09:53.320
Yeah, it would just be, oh, you'd find out about it in a table read.

190
00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:59.400
Or maybe somebody would come to your trailer and be like, hey, tomorrow I'm going to need you to play this.

191
00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.880
Or maybe the moment, like you're saying, like for Goodbye Tobey.

192
00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:11.900
Yeah, but the Dunder Mifflin song, you know, BJ's brother wrote the original, the Dunder Mifflin, the paper version.

193
00:10:12.500 --> 00:10:16.380
And then they gave it to me to kind of flip it, you know, for the story.

194
00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:20.220
And I got, I think I did that like the night before.

195
00:10:20.360 --> 00:10:21.020
So that was cool.

196
00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:22.440
Had a little time for that.

197
00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:24.900
But, yeah, sometimes it just be.

198
00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:26.580
That is amazing.

199
00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:34.080
You know, you and I were at Creed Bratton's show, and you started playing that on the piano, and the place went berserk.

200
00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:39.240
I mean, the minute you sat down to the keyboard and just started, they all started singing.

201
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:40.180
They knew immediately.

202
00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:41.060
It was so cool.

203
00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:43.760
I mean, Craig, I never get to be on stage in a band.

204
00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:45.380
And that was, I had the tambourine.

205
00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.080
I think someone handed me a tambourine.

206
00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:50.600
That was the closest, like, moment to feeling like a rock star.

207
00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:51.820
I thought it was so fun.

208
00:10:51.820 --> 00:11:00.400
Well, just being on the office, and you got to be a rock star at least two times in your life from going to Scranton.

209
00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.860
Those two times, you already know.

210
00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:03.600
Yeah.

211
00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.360
Because they lost their minds.

212
00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:09.480
But, yeah, sometimes I'll play it at the show, my comedy shows, out of paper.

213
00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:15.840
And then, like, whether it's a group or just one person knowing it, it's always satisfying.

214
00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:22.320
Because, I mean, how many times do you have to have watched the series to know that song and know the words, you know, specifically?

215
00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:23.180
Yeah.

216
00:11:23.580 --> 00:11:25.920
Well, we know you also have an improv background.

217
00:11:25.960 --> 00:11:28.200
You studied at Second City in Chicago.

218
00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:36.480
Is there an improvised moment that you can remember from the show that you particularly love?

219
00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:43.660
Angela always says if an improviser gets a moment into a TV show or a movie, you never forget it.

220
00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:44.460
You never forget it.

221
00:11:44.460 --> 00:11:46.120
So, we are curious if you had one.

222
00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:50.600
Oh, I mean, I have several in a negotiation.

223
00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:56.040
And I was telling Mike, I was like, yeah, come on, go get that raise and make it happen, Captain.

224
00:11:56.540 --> 00:11:58.520
And I said that, make it happen, Captain.

225
00:11:58.820 --> 00:11:59.220
Right?

226
00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:03.900
And then Steve turned around, and I didn't realize this until I watched it.

227
00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:05.620
He said, I will.

228
00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:06.520
He got the phone.

229
00:12:06.620 --> 00:12:08.900
So, he was like, I will make it happen, Sergeant.

230
00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.340
And I was like, oh, wow, I didn't even see that happen on set.

231
00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:14.860
Okay.

232
00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:16.960
That's awesome.

233
00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:20.720
Well, I have a favorite Daryl moment from this rewatch.

234
00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.980
It's season three, Back from Vacation.

235
00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:32.400
That's the one where Michael realizes that he accidentally emailed the topless photo of Jan to everybody in the whole office.

236
00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:33.360
Start over.

237
00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:39.260
And he runs down to the warehouse because he wants you to delete it from your computer.

238
00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:45.820
And when he gets in there, you're sitting there, you have your feet up on the desk, and you're eating a plate of food.

239
00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:48.720
And you say, I don't know, I'm really busy, Michael.

240
00:12:50.480 --> 00:12:57.540
And I went, when I saw that moment, I went and I checked the script, and there is no mention that Daryl is eating a plate of food.

241
00:12:58.120 --> 00:13:06.180
And so, I asked writer Justin Spitzer about it, and he said that that was your idea, that you went over to Crafty, and you grabbed a plate of food before the scene started.

242
00:13:07.060 --> 00:13:13.400
Yeah, I wanted to look as not busy, but to say I was busy is possible.

243
00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:14.780
So, yeah.

244
00:13:15.480 --> 00:13:19.080
Well, the reason I was suspicious was because I looked at the food on the plate.

245
00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:20.180
And you recognized the food.

246
00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:22.880
And I was like, that looks a lot like our hot snack.

247
00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:26.580
I mean, that looks like right after 1030.

248
00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:28.000
That's not still shade.

249
00:13:28.420 --> 00:13:30.980
That was, you know, a grab and go.

250
00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:31.980
Yeah, that's right.

251
00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:36.920
Oh, speaking of hot snack, do you have a favorite hot snack?

252
00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:39.820
I would kill the pastrami sandwich.

253
00:13:40.340 --> 00:13:41.040
Oh, yeah.

254
00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:45.960
That was, for those who don't know, they would, the way they fed us on that show.

255
00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:46.640
Mm-hmm.

256
00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:51.540
Did breakfast in the morning, three hours later, a hot snack, three hours later, lunch, three hours later, hot snack.

257
00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:54.280
And then a lot of times, there was second meal.

258
00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.980
It was torturous and wonderful.

259
00:13:58.260 --> 00:13:59.140
And wonderful.

260
00:13:59.140 --> 00:14:09.860
I found an email thread in my digital clutter where we were all going back and forth as a cast about the Albandegas soup that Bartan would put out.

261
00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:13.160
There was a whole email thread about it because I guess one week we didn't get it.

262
00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:14.660
And everyone was like, where's the soup?

263
00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:19.840
I remember the first time they put out turkey chili and then we wanted it every day.

264
00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:20.240
Oh, yeah.

265
00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:23.440
That was John's favorite, I think, and Ed.

266
00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:26.420
Well, we talk a lot about behind-the-scenes moments.

267
00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:31.580
You know, obviously, Jenna and I, we are watching this and remembering all kinds of stuff.

268
00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:36.160
But do you have a favorite behind-the-scenes moment where we would mess with each other or just do anything?

269
00:14:37.060 --> 00:14:38.400
Man, oh, my God.

270
00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:47.760
You know, I was thoroughly entertained coming to work every day watching, just sitting back watching y'all for the most part.

271
00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:54.120
Do you remember when, what was the one, when Rain was on the tightrope?

272
00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:55.420
Yes!

273
00:14:55.960 --> 00:14:57.080
Oh, my God.

274
00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:00.180
That still haunts me to this day.

275
00:15:00.340 --> 00:15:01.740
Him trying to balance.

276
00:15:01.940 --> 00:15:02.220
Yeah.

277
00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.960
And we were all just laughing, trying to hold our laugh in.

278
00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:08.660
But I just remember the laughter on his face.

279
00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:09.380
Oh, man.

280
00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:13.420
I have found some old videos from my, I mean, old phone.

281
00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:14.720
Remember the phones we had?

282
00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:17.580
Like the, you could kind of video off a flip phone?

283
00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:18.000
Yeah.

284
00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:24.220
And I have a real grainy video of you and Ed and Kate singing your hearts out.

285
00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:28.060
And Ellie, it was like in between scenes for a Halloween episode.

286
00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:31.420
And Craig, I mean, I texted it to you.

287
00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.620
Did you get to see it, like, ages ago?

288
00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:35.380
No, I don't remember that.

289
00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:35.900
What?

290
00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:37.840
Oh, my gosh!

291
00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:42.260
Oh, I'm sending it, because you guys are having so much fun.

292
00:15:42.700 --> 00:15:43.220
Oh, no.

293
00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:44.340
Who did I text it to?

294
00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.280
I might not have your correct number anymore.

295
00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:50.140
Oh, crap.

296
00:15:50.300 --> 00:15:51.400
Email me your phone number.

297
00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:53.080
You might be my old number.

298
00:15:53.220 --> 00:15:58.640
Somebody has my old number, and they're, I guess, very polite telling people, hey, he doesn't,

299
00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.140
this isn't his number anymore.

300
00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:04.120
I should probably coordinate with them and say, hey, could you send them this way?

301
00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:04.820
Yeah.

302
00:16:05.820 --> 00:16:06.300
All right.

303
00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:07.720
I've got a question for you, Craig.

304
00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:12.800
Is there a Daryl quote that fans say to you when they see you out?

305
00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:17.740
Like, for example, people always shout, save Bandit, at Angela.

306
00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:18.700
Oh, my gosh, Craig.

307
00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:20.400
So many people want me to save Bandit.

308
00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:22.740
Dinkin' Flicka all day long.

309
00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:24.200
Dinkin' Flicka?

310
00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:27.300
Dibbity-bobbity, gimme-na-zobbity all day long.

311
00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:27.760
Yes.

312
00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:29.140
For sure.

313
00:16:30.120 --> 00:16:33.180
Well, did you keep anything from the set when we wrapped?

314
00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:35.060
Because we all took a little something.

315
00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:38.120
You know, I think I took that little monkey.

316
00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:39.860
You remember that little monkey over there?

317
00:16:39.860 --> 00:16:42.840
You know who was obsessed with that little monkey was my daughter, Isabel.

318
00:16:43.140 --> 00:16:45.140
She would go and find it and squeeze it.

319
00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.260
And then it would, you know, it made so much noise.

320
00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:50.760
How is Isabel?

321
00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:51.800
Oh, my goodness.

322
00:16:51.940 --> 00:16:52.600
How old is she now?

323
00:16:52.600 --> 00:16:53.800
She's 14.

324
00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:55.040
What?

325
00:16:55.360 --> 00:16:56.480
Oh, my gosh.

326
00:16:56.620 --> 00:16:59.900
It freaks people out that worked with us back then when they see her now.

327
00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.400
Jeez, yeah, no kidding.

328
00:17:01.660 --> 00:17:02.900
She was a little kid on set.

329
00:17:03.160 --> 00:17:04.740
I have to bring this up, Craig.

330
00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:08.800
I loved you so much as Doug Judy on Brooklyn Nine-Nine.

331
00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:11.180
That is such a phenomenal character.

332
00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.860
If you guys haven't watched Craig on Brooklyn Nine-Nine, you've got to go see it.

333
00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:18.180
I mean, I lived for him.

334
00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:19.800
Thank you.

335
00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:20.240
Thank you.

336
00:17:20.300 --> 00:17:22.520
That was so much fun.

337
00:17:22.600 --> 00:17:27.180
You know, I was doing that and The Office, because every year they had me do an episode or whatever.

338
00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:35.500
But, yeah, Andy Samberg, I don't know if y'all know Andy, but, like, working with him, it was like two kids coming together.

339
00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.680
You ever see two kids just coming together and just talking to the band?

340
00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:39.440
It was that, for real.

341
00:17:39.600 --> 00:17:41.420
It has that energy, actually.

342
00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:42.440
It really does.

343
00:17:42.880 --> 00:17:46.700
I mean, I just re-watched you guys in the convertible singing about the shoes.

344
00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:49.320
It's like, that is so great.

345
00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:51.980
I guarantee it was an improvised song.

346
00:17:51.980 --> 00:17:55.880
I'm putting that in our office ladies' Insta stories because it's so good.

347
00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:58.700
Is there anything you're working on now that we can share?

348
00:17:58.920 --> 00:17:59.360
Yes.

349
00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:00.160
Yes, I am.

350
00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:00.980
I'm on the road right now.

351
00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:06.280
I have a show September 22nd in Brooklyn, at Brooklyn Bowl, if anybody's interested in that.

352
00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:12.180
And then coming up, let me see, the 23rd, Governors, Long Island.

353
00:18:12.180 --> 00:18:16.020
And then the 25th, Toronto, JFL, Just for Laughs.

354
00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:24.320
But other than that, we're going to start shooting season two of my show on streaming service, Peacock TV.

355
00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:25.150
Woo!

356
00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.340
Killing it is so good.

357
00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:27.860
Thank you.

358
00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:31.680
Jen and I got to watch the pilot just the other day, and it is so funny.

359
00:18:31.720 --> 00:18:34.260
And I did not see that second part coming.

360
00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:35.740
I don't want to give anything away.

361
00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:42.020
Oh, there's some things coming in that's going to make your mouth drop a little bit.

362
00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:43.300
But they're very funny.

363
00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:46.700
Craig, who is the Australian actress who stars in that?

364
00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:48.120
Bonnie O'Doherty.

365
00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:49.960
Oh, I love you two together.

366
00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:51.040
She's so good.

367
00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:52.600
She's brilliant.

368
00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:58.160
Her sense of self and sense of timing and comedy, she's so great to work with.

369
00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:00.440
Do you guys improv a lot on that show?

370
00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:03.980
We get some chances for sure to improv.

371
00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:08.260
And yeah, we have some fun making stuff up.

372
00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:11.120
You guys are an amazing duo.

373
00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:16.960
Like, your chemistry, I just want you two to be stuck in a car all the time, basically.

374
00:19:18.120 --> 00:19:24.320
It was actually very similar to, like, the Michael Scott-Daryl-stuck-in-a-car energy.

375
00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:25.320
It was just awkward.

376
00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:26.400
Just awkward.

377
00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:26.520
Yeah.

378
00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:27.940
So good.

379
00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:35.420
But in the show, in Killing It, it's so great because you get the opportunity to be the funny

380
00:19:35.420 --> 00:19:37.240
man and the straight man.

381
00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:37.700
And the straight man.

382
00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:41.240
We saw so much of the straight man side of you on The Office.

383
00:19:41.440 --> 00:19:45.140
And Killing It, I feel like we get the full Craig Robinson.

384
00:19:45.860 --> 00:19:52.540
Yeah, it's definitely opened me up a little bit to showcase some different sides.

385
00:19:52.740 --> 00:19:56.240
So I'm appreciative and I love, love the show.

386
00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:57.140
I love doing it.

387
00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:59.180
I love, I love going to work.

388
00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:01.740
We were shooting in New Orleans and now we're going to shoot in LA.

389
00:20:02.340 --> 00:20:04.580
So that's fun and interesting.

390
00:20:05.180 --> 00:20:05.500
Yeah.

391
00:20:05.500 --> 00:20:11.460
Well, if you need two middle-aged ladies to just, you know, do a pop-in on Killing It,

392
00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:12.680
we're available.

393
00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:14.660
Come on down.

394
00:20:15.060 --> 00:20:16.600
I'm sure you're possible for you.

395
00:20:17.520 --> 00:20:23.740
And also, Craig, I saw on your website, MrCraigRobinson.com, all of your show dates and everything up to

396
00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:24.000
date.

397
00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:26.520
So whoever's doing your website is doing a real good job.

398
00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:29.460
So if you guys want to catch Craig, go to his website.

399
00:20:29.760 --> 00:20:31.500
My God, it's so good to see you.

400
00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:32.440
Y'all too.

401
00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:33.780
Thank you so much.

402
00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:34.620
Yeah.

403
00:20:34.900 --> 00:20:36.580
Craig, is there anything that we missed?

404
00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:40.900
Anything else you'd like to share about your time on The Office or what it's meant to you?

405
00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:47.400
I mean, you know, it's like being on The Office, it's like you're everybody's friend.

406
00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:51.160
Like everywhere I go, somebody's ready to buy a drink or share a story.

407
00:20:51.380 --> 00:20:57.040
Some stories I get, like it's the only time my family comes together is or my mother was sick

408
00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:58.580
and this is the only thing made her laugh.

409
00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:03.520
You know, it's all these incredible stories about people bonding with The Office and now

410
00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:10.320
people have seen it, you know, 9, 10, 14 times through and somebody's like, it's on constant

411
00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:11.140
loop in my house.

412
00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:15.060
It's just such a blessing to be a part of that.

413
00:21:15.240 --> 00:21:19.800
And, you know, it's a blessing to share with you ladies.

414
00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:21.500
You know, we had some good times over.

415
00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:22.620
Yeah, we did.

416
00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:24.500
Well, Craig, we love you.

417
00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:26.560
It was so good to see you.

418
00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:28.640
Thank you so much for having me, ladies.

419
00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:33.020
Everybody around the world are like, do office ladies, do office ladies.

420
00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:34.280
I'm like, I'm trying, I'm trying.

421
00:21:36.340 --> 00:21:37.400
Well, we love you.

422
00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:38.300
Big hugs.

423
00:21:38.660 --> 00:21:39.600
Big hugs, Craig.

424
00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:43.100
I want to see you in person soon.

425
00:21:43.600 --> 00:21:44.640
Yes, just let me know.

426
00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:45.380
All right.

427
00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:45.840
All right.

428
00:21:46.060 --> 00:21:46.220
All right.

429
00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:46.800
Yeah, be good.

430
00:21:47.200 --> 00:21:47.740
Thank you.

431
00:21:57.890 --> 00:21:58.870
We are back.

432
00:21:58.870 --> 00:22:00.930
It was so awesome talking to Craig.

433
00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:01.990
My gosh.

434
00:22:02.210 --> 00:22:03.490
Oh, I love him.

435
00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:04.210
I love him.

436
00:22:04.570 --> 00:22:09.050
To start this episode, Sam, Cassie, Jenna, I have a question for you.

437
00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:09.870
What?

438
00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:10.690
Yeah.

439
00:22:10.970 --> 00:22:11.210
Okay.

440
00:22:11.430 --> 00:22:11.690
I have a question for you.

441
00:22:11.910 --> 00:22:15.250
Do you want more freedom, less freedom, or to stay the same?

442
00:22:16.470 --> 00:22:19.030
Um, I think I always want more freedom.

443
00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:20.090
You are a Danny.

444
00:22:20.910 --> 00:22:22.190
I'm also going to go with more.

445
00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:23.470
You are a Danny.

446
00:22:24.090 --> 00:22:25.130
I'm a Danny.

447
00:22:25.670 --> 00:22:27.010
Danny's all around.

448
00:22:27.330 --> 00:22:28.350
What about you, Ange?

449
00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:29.310
I think I'm a Danny.

450
00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:29.930
Okay.

451
00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:31.230
Don't mess with my stuff.

452
00:22:31.370 --> 00:22:32.230
I want my freedom.

453
00:22:32.570 --> 00:22:33.830
Well, let me ask you this.

454
00:22:33.950 --> 00:22:39.090
Do you guys want a better life, a worse life, or do you want your life to stay the same?

455
00:22:39.670 --> 00:22:40.710
I'm going to say better.

456
00:22:41.030 --> 00:22:41.950
You're going with better?

457
00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:42.970
I'm going to have to get cocky.

458
00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:44.530
Cassie left the room.

459
00:22:44.630 --> 00:22:46.250
I'm going to assume she was going to go for better.

460
00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:46.710
Okay.

461
00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:48.610
I like my life.

462
00:22:48.890 --> 00:22:50.150
You want it to stay the same?

463
00:22:50.230 --> 00:22:50.470
Yeah.

464
00:22:50.610 --> 00:22:51.730
I'm happy in my life.

465
00:22:52.170 --> 00:22:55.890
But I mean, wouldn't better just be even better than the life you already like?

466
00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:57.110
I'm happy.

467
00:22:57.310 --> 00:22:57.910
I am too.

468
00:22:57.910 --> 00:23:00.930
I mean, listen, if my life stayed the same, I wouldn't be upset.

469
00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:02.230
Yeah.

470
00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:02.810
I don't know.

471
00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:07.690
That's a tricky one because it's like, I'm so thankful for what I have.

472
00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:09.070
I don't want to be too greedy.

473
00:23:09.230 --> 00:23:09.850
I'm so happy.

474
00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:10.890
Okay.

475
00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:13.890
So we'll keep the life we have, but with more freedom.

476
00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:14.530
Yeah.

477
00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:15.350
We're half Danny.

478
00:23:15.650 --> 00:23:16.570
We're half Danny.

479
00:23:19.870 --> 00:23:23.310
This episode opens with Oscar in the parking lot.

480
00:23:23.470 --> 00:23:24.190
He has a new bike.

481
00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:25.270
He's a cycliSt.

482
00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:25.770
Who knew?

483
00:23:25.770 --> 00:23:26.510
Who knew?

484
00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:28.270
Did you catch the PAM sass?

485
00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:29.370
I did.

486
00:23:29.590 --> 00:23:31.110
It was so delicious to me.

487
00:23:31.190 --> 00:23:32.770
I really felt like we had to hear it.

488
00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:34.210
Sam, can you please play it?

489
00:23:34.810 --> 00:23:35.610
Love cycling.

490
00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:37.850
Andy, I feel like a tourist in my own city.

491
00:23:38.010 --> 00:23:39.750
I literally can't wait to wake up every morning.

492
00:23:39.890 --> 00:23:40.130
Okay.

493
00:23:40.370 --> 00:23:40.950
She is a lot.

494
00:23:40.970 --> 00:23:41.230
Cute.

495
00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:42.170
Can't beat a horse.

